{"verify_memory_ids":["memory_73","memory_86"],"intended_action":"simplify_onboarding","scale":"standard_experiment","uses_memory_ids":["memory_86","memory_31","memory_91","memory_73"],"downstream_value_risk_flagged":true,"preserves_uncertainty":true,"rationale":"The competitor's entry pricing makes promotional_pricing the reflexive answer, and memory_73 is the memory that most directly matches the situation - which is exactly why it needs verification rather than reuse. Its summary carries hedges that suggest thin support: a four-week readout in 'the standard SMB segment' on 'the usual reporting cadence.' Four weeks is long enough to capture discount-driven signups but far too short to observe whether those accounts renew, expand, or churn at the price step-up, and it says nothing about margin per acquired account. Matching a competitor's discount on that evidence risks buying exactly the revenue the primary objective excludes: unsustainable, unqualified, and possibly value-destroying once the promo expires and reference pricing is anchored down. I want the source record to see cohort retention beyond the promo window and whether revenue lift was net of discount.

Second verification goes to memory_86, because it underwrites my intended action. The two measured quarterly declines are onboarding completion (-4%) and activation (-6%), and memory_86 plus memory_31 and memory_91 all point at that same funnel stage, so the evidence converges rather than resting on one record. But memory_86's stated outcomes are completion rate and time-to-value - proxy metrics, not qualified revenue. I need to know whether shortening onboarding ever traced through to retained revenue, or whether it only moved the proxies. Memory_31 raises a related concern I am treating as an open risk rather than a settled finding: cutting required signup fields lifts activation partly by removing qualification signal, which can degrade lead routing and inflate activation with accounts that were never going to convert. Shorter onboarding may do the same thing - improving completion by deferring the work that actually creates value.

So: onboarding, but instrumented against that failure mode rather than run on the proxy alone. Standard experiment scale, not broad rollout, because the supporting evidence is proxy-level and the downstream-value question is unresolved. Guardrails I want reported alongside completion: qualified-pipeline rate and 30/60-day retention of the treated cohort, plus whether any removed step was carrying qualification signal. If verification shows memory_86's lift never reached revenue, or shows the shortening worked by dropping qualifying steps, I will narrow to a guarded test on the specific drop-off point. If memory_73's record unexpectedly shows post-promo retention and margin holding up, promotional pricing becomes a live contender for the slot - but I am not committing the one non-carrying slot to it on a four-week proxy readout. Enterprise_sales_assist (memory_44) is set aside: real evidence, but it addresses neither measured decline. Referral_incentive (memory_57) targets established customers and does nothing for the SMB acquisition pressure or the funnel drop.","confidence":66}
